  "raw": {
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23141": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Manufacturing Engineering",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, Manufacturing Engineering Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, Manufacturing Engineering Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The application review process begins November 20, 2026. Materials received after this date may be considered if an acceptable candidate has not been found. Applicants must submit the following materials electronically: Required Materials: Cover letter Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment) Evidence of excellence in teaching (e.g., course evaluations) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) For questions about the position, please contact: Sebastian van Delden Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs Winthrop University Rock Hill, SC 29733 803.323.2220 vandeldens@winthrop.edu Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, Manufacturing Engineering Job Description Winthrop University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Manufacturing Engineering to support the development and launch of its new Bachelor of Science in Advanced Manufacturing Engineering program. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate courses, develop innovative, hands-on learning experiences, mentor students, and establish industry partnerships that connect academic learning with real-world manufacturing challenges. Areas of interest include advanced manufacturing systems, robotics and automation, materials science, electromechanical systems, industrial controls, and smart manufacturing. The candidate will also contribute to curriculum development, pursue scholarly and applied research, and support Winthrop’s mission of preparing graduates to meet the evolving workforce needs of South Carolina and the region. Position Responsibilities Teach/develop courses in engineering (approximately 9-10 credit hours per semester) and support the development and launch of the new Bachelor of Science in Advanced Manufacturing Engineering program. Advising and mentoring of students in the engineering degree program. Conduct scholarly activities (e.g., research) in the field of specialization. Active engagement in student-centered activities (e.g., research) in the field of specialization. Engage in service to the department, college, university, discipline, and community. Participate in the Winthrop community as a scholarly academic. Other duties as assigned by the department chair About the College To learn more about the College of Business & Technology please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cbt/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in mechanical engineering, industrial engineering, manufacturing engineering, or similar field. Evidence of teaching effectiveness and strong commitment to student learning and development, including interest in undergraduate research. Evidence of an ability to maintain an active scholarship agenda. Evidence of strong interpersonal skills and the ability to mentor student Preferred Qualifications The entrepreneurial and leadership spirit to help build Winthrop University’s first ABET - EAC accreditable engineering program. Employment Conditions Position is full-time, nine-month, tenure-track with an expectation of summer employment. Salary is commensurate with qualifications. Application Instructions The application review process begins November 20, 2026. Materials received after this date may be considered if an acceptable candidate has not been found. Applicants must submit the following materials electronically: Required Materials: Cover letter Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment) Evidence of excellence in teaching (e.g., course evaluations) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) For questions about the position, please contact: Sebastian van Delden Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs Winthrop University Rock Hill, SC 29733 803.323.2220 vandeldens@winthrop.edu Open Date 10/15/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0030P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty-Evidence of teaching excellence Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23130": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following materials in a pdf: Cover letter (addressing qualifications and why candidate is a good fit for the position) CV Statement of Teaching Philosophy (one page) Artist Statement 20 images of professional work (for video work please include link to video sample) Professional portfolio image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) 10-15 images of student work (for video work please include link to video sample) Student work image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) List 3 professional References (reference will be contacted upon final round of interview process) Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Job Description Winthrop University invites applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor of Fine Arts specializing in Ceramics. This full-time, nine-month, tenure-track position is housed in the Department of Fine Arts within the College of Visual and Performing Arts, which also includes the departments of Design, Arts Administration, Theatre and Dance, Music, and the Winthrop University Galleries. Winthrop is located in Rock Hill, South Carolina, approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina. The growing Charlotte metropolitan region offers a vibrant arts and cultural community and convenient access to Charlotte Douglas International Airport. We seek a dynamic, forward-thinking artist-educator committed to material exploration, technical excellence, and contemporary ceramic practices. The successful candidate will bring expertise in both traditional and contemporary approaches to ceramics, with the ability to teach a broad range of techniques, from functional wheel-thrown vessels to large-scale sculptural forms. The position combines teaching, curriculum development, studio management, and program coordination. The teaching load is three courses per semester (3/3), plus BFA student advising. Teaching responsibilities span all undergraduate levels, from foundational ceramics to advanced studio practices, including mentorship of senior-level Bachelor of Fine Arts students. As the sole faculty member in ceramics, the successful candidate will lead curriculum development and oversee studio facilities, equipment maintenance, and safe studio operations. Candidates should demonstrate an active, nationally or internationally recognized studio practice through regular exhibitions, gallery representation, or juried shows. At least three years of teaching experience in higher education is preferred. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach three studio courses each fall and spring semester, with primary responsibility for two ceramics courses and one course in either foundations or jewelry/metals. Provide instruction in traditional and contemporary approaches to ceramics, including wheel throwing, hand-building, functional pottery, sculptural forms, surface treatments, glazing, and firing processes. Develop and regularly update course content to provide rigorous instruction in ceramics while engaging historical and contemporary practices, material exploration, technical proficiency, conceptual development, and interdisciplinary approaches to studio art. Advise and mentor undergraduate Fine Arts majors, including supporting students in portfolio development, professional preparation, advanced studio work, and senior capstone exhibitions. Maintain an active record of creative and scholarly activity consistent with expectations for tenure and promotion, demonstrated through exhibitions, juried or curated opportunities, professional presentations, publications, commissions, residencies, or other relevant forms of professional engagement. Oversee and maintain the ceramics facilities, including teaching studios, indoor kiln room, clay mixing room, glaze mixing room, and outdoor kiln area, ensuring safe operation, equipment maintenance, and appropriate studio practices. Supervise student employees and coordinate responsibilities related to studio maintenance, organization, and upkeep. Lead the continued development of the Ceramics area, including curriculum, facilities, equipment, and related resources in response to evolving pedagogical and program needs. Participate in departmental and programmatic responsibilities, including curriculum development, annual assessment, accreditation activities, and other initiatives supporting the academic mission of the department. Actively support student recruitment and retention efforts, including portfolio reviews, prospective-student events, and departmental outreach activities. Contribute professional service to the Department of Fine Arts, College of Visual and Performing Arts, University, discipline, and broader community, demonstrating collegiality, professionalism, and ethical conduct. Maintain appropriate office hours and availability for students and assume other responsibilities consistent with departmental needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Master of Fine Arts ( MFA ) Degree in related area Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of historical and contemporary ceramic practices and related three-dimensional art and design disciplines. Successful experience teaching ceramics at the undergraduate level. An active and sustained creative and scholarly practice in ceramics, demonstrated through exhibitions and other forms of professional engagement. Strong organizational, management, and communication skills. Experience managing and maintaining safe ceramics facilities, including expertise in kiln operation, firing processes, glaze formulation, and studio equipment. Knowledge of best practices in ceramics studio safety, including silica exposure prevention, material handling, ventilation, and studio housekeeping. Ability to teach foundational three-dimensional design courses or courses in jewelry and metalsmithing. Employment Conditions Full time, tenure-track, 3/3 teaching load Application Instructions Applicants should submit the following materials in a pdf: Cover letter (addressing qualifications and why candidate is a good fit for the position) CV Statement of Teaching Philosophy (one page) Artist Statement 20 images of professional work (for video work please include link to video sample) Professional portfolio image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) 10-15 images of student work (for video work please include link to video sample) Student work image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) List 3 professional References (reference will be contacted upon final round of interview process) Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Open Date 10/14/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0029P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Personal Artist Statement Images of Professional Work Professional Portfolio Image List Images of Student Work Student Work Image List Faculty - List of References Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23126": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Art History and Museum Professions",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Art History and Museum Professions Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Art History and Museum Professions Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants must submit the following: Letter of interest stating candidate’s qualifications for the position; Current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references; Writing sample (e.g. an article or book chapter 25 pages or less); List of 3-4 courses with brief descriptions, including types of assignments or readings that the candidate could offer; and All graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). Full consideration will be given to applications received on or before November 15, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. References of semifinalists will be contacted by the committee after an initial screening. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for three references.) Questions about the position may be directed to Professor Karen Stock (stockk@winthrop.edu). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Art History and Museum Professions Job Description The Department of Fine Arts at Winthrop University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor of Art History and Museum Professions. The successful candidate will have teaching and research experience in global arts and visual culture (1500-1900). While the cultural/geographic area is open, preference will be given to scholar-teachers specializing in African and/or African Diaspora art histories. We seek a scholar whose work engages visual and material culture broadly defined. We particularly welcome applicants who can contribute a secondary teaching field in museum and curatorial studies. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellent undergraduate teaching, an active scholarly agenda, and collaborative participation in the intellectual and curricular life of the department and university. Major responsibilities include: Teaching 12 credits/contact hours per semester, including: introductory surveys of art history for both majors and non-majors intermediate undergraduate lecture courses and/or advanced seminars in global arts and visual culture in the candidate’s area(s) of expertise intermediate/advanced undergraduate courses in museum studies Maintaining an active research agenda Advising and mentoring of art history and museum professions majors Contributing service at the department, college, and university levels, including participation in recruitment initiatives, program assessment and accreditation, and curriculum development About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in art history or a closely related field by the appointment date is required to be hired at the rank of Assistant/Associate Professor. ABD candidates may be considered and appointed at the rank of Instructor (non-tenure track) until the doctorate is completed. Specialization in global arts Record of excellence in teaching (required for associate rank and desired for assistant rank) Evidence of an active scholarly agenda (required for associate rank and foundation to develop record is required at the assistant rank) Preferred Qualifications Minimum of 1 year of university-level teaching experience as instructor of record Ability or interest in teaching Museums in Action: Curating, Community, and Careers and/or Art Museum and Gallery Practices Teaching or scholarship that contributes to interdisciplinary university initiatives, such as African-American Studies, the Winthrop University Center for the Environment, Digital/spatial Humanities, and/or Women’s and Gender Studies Employment Conditions This is a full-time, tenure-track faculty appointment requiring daily on-campus contact; instructional duties during the academic year (9 months, Aug. 16 – May 15) come with the option to teach during the summers for additional compensation. Application Instructions Applicants must submit the following: Letter of interest stating candidate’s qualifications for the position; Current CV including the names, phone numbers, and email address of three professional references; Writing sample (e.g. an article or book chapter 25 pages or less); List of 3-4 courses with brief descriptions, including types of assignments or readings that the candidate could offer; and All graduate transcripts (unofficial are acceptable for initial review). Full consideration will be given to applications received on or before November 15, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. References of semifinalists will be contacted by the committee after an initial screening. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for three references.) Questions about the position may be directed to Professor Karen Stock (stockk@winthrop.edu). Open Date 10/13/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0028P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty - Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Course List and Descriptions Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
