    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23107": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Exercise Science",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor, Exercise Science Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor, Exercise Science Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application review will begin on October 30, 2026. Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Applications should include: A cover letter including the candidate’s interest in the position, qualifications, leadership experience, teaching philosophy, and research interests is required. Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Copy of CSCS credential Additional materials may be requested during the review process. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Specific questions related to this search should be directed to Dr. Janet Wojcik at wojcikj@winthrop.edu. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor, Exercise Science Job Description The Department of Physical Education, Sport, and Human Performance ( PESH ) at Winthrop University is seeking an innovative and involved tenure-track assistant professor to serve as Internship and Field Experience Coordinator and teach in an established Bachelor of Science in Exercise Science program. The program is accredited by the Commission on Accreditation of Allied Health Education Programs ( CAAHEP ) and the Commission on Accreditation of Exercise Science (CoAES). The program will be seeking accreditation with Council on Accreditation of Strength and Conditioning Education ( CASCE ). The individual will collaborate with other faculty to ensure that undergraduate students interested in pursuing exercise science receive high-quality didactic education and exceptional internship and field experiences. We seek a candidate who will be an exemplary leader, teacher, scholar, and active participant in the college, on campus, with community partners, and in professional organizations. Position Responsibilities Serve as the Internship and Field Experience Coordinator for the Exercise Science program; Maintain comprehensive records of student internship and field experiences; Teach courses in exercise science, strength and conditioning, health, and other related fields at the undergraduate and graduate levels; Support the vision for program initiatives, curriculum development, and accreditation; Serve as a productive faculty member in all areas of university expectations (student intellectual development, scholarly activity, professional stewardship, and academic responsibility); Actively engage in student recruitment and selection processes; Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students; Foster collaborative relationships with sport, allied health, and other organizations to develop internship and field experience placements and opportunities for collaborative service to the profession; Use technology to enhance student learning, increase access, maintain program data, and facilitate recruitment and retention efforts; and Engage in teaching and other program-related activities in the evenings, online, and occasional weekends, as needed. About the College To learn more about the Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/ceshs/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Doctorate with specialization in exercise science, strength and conditioning or closely related field ( ABD with completion by summer 2027 will be considered); Certified Strength and Conditioning Specialist ( CSCS ) credential; Experience as a provider of exercise science or strength and conditioning services to clients or patients; Foundation for successful collegiate teaching; Foundation for active scholarship; and Ability to work collaboratively with departmental and other university members Preferred Qualifications Experience with CASCE and/or CoAES accreditation procedures; Experience supervising exercise science or strength and conditioning internship and field experiences; Demonstrated ability in the use of technology and online learning for instructional purposes; Demonstrated ability to use technology for student record keeping and management; Experience working with underrepresented students and students from at-risk backgrounds; and Expertise in one or more of the following areas of exercise science or strength and conditioning: exercise physiology, biomechanics, kinesiology, exercise prescription, athlete assessment and performance, and military and tactical athlete performance. Employment Conditions Position is full-time, nine-month, tenure-track with the possibility of summer employment. Salary is commensurate with qualifications. Application Instructions Application review will begin on October 30, 2026. Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Applications should include: A cover letter including the candidate’s interest in the position, qualifications, leadership experience, teaching philosophy, and research interests is required. Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Copy of CSCS credential Additional materials may be requested during the review process. Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Specific questions related to this search should be directed to Dr. Janet Wojcik at wojcikj@winthrop.edu. Open Date 09/23/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0024P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts License or Certificate Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23084": {
      "title": "Assistant and Associate Professor, Athletic Training",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant and Associate Professor, Athletic Training Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant and Associate Professor, Athletic Training Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Application review will begin on October 30, 2026. Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Applications should include: Cover letter including the candidate’s interest in the position, desired rank, interest in clinical experience leadership role, applicable qualifications, and leadership experience. Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Detailed statement of teaching philosophy. Statement of research interests; Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Additional materials may be requested during the review process. Specific questions related to this search should be directed to Dr. Alice McLaine at mclainea@winthrop.edu . Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant and Associate Professor, Athletic Training Job Description The Department of Physical Education, Sport, and Human Performance ( PESH ) at Winthrop University is seeking two faculty in Athletic Training. Through this search we seek innovative and engaged tenure-track faculty. One position is at the associate professor rank and the second is at the assistant professor rank. Both faculty will teach in an established and evolving Master of Science in Athletic Training program. One position will include the Director of Clinical Education responsibilities. The program is accredited by the Commission on Accreditation of Athletic Training Education ( CAATE ). The individuals will collaborate with other faculty to ensure that graduate students and undergraduate students interested in pursuing athletic training receive high-quality didactic education, practice remains congruent with disciplinary standards, and clinical experiences meet the needs of health care professionals in training. We seek candidates who will be exemplary leaders, teachers, scholars, and active participants in the college, on campus, with community partners, and professional organizations. Position Responsibilities Teach courses in athletic training, exercise science, and health at the graduate and undergraduate levels; Contribute to program initiatives, curriculum development, and accreditation; Serve as a productive faculty member in all areas of university expectations (student intellectual development, scholarly activity, professional stewardship, and academic responsibility); Lead efforts to develop and maintain strong cohorts, including recruitment and selection processes; Advise and mentor undergraduate and graduate students; Foster collaborative relationships with medical and sport organizations to develop clinical experience placements and opportunities for service to the profession.; Use technology to enhance student learning, increase access, maintain program data, and facilitate recruitment and retention efforts; and Engage in teaching and other program-related activities in the evenings, summers (this is a 12-month program), online, and occasional weekends, as needed. About the College To learn more about the Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/ceshs/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Doctorate with specialization in athletic training or closely related field ( ABD with completion by summer 2027 will be considered); Current Board of Certification ( BOC ) credential; Eligible for licensure as an athletic trainer in South Carolina; Experience as a provider of athletic training services to scholastic, intercollegiate, or professional athletes or other clinical sites; Record of successful collegiate teaching (required for associate rank and desired for assistant rank); Record of scholarly / creative work (required for associate rank and foundation to develop record is required at the assistant rank); Ability to work collaboratively with department colleagues and other members of the university community Preferred Qualifications Experience with CAATE accreditation procedures; Previous experience teaching in a CAATE -accredited program; Experience supervising and/or establishing athletic training clinical experiences; Demonstrated ability in the use of simulation, technology, and online learning for instructional purposes; Demonstrated ability to use technology for student record keeping and management; Experience working with underrepresented students and students from at-risk backgrounds; Expertise in one or more of the following areas: evidence-based practice, therapeutic interventions, administration, research and writing, and the implementation of standardized patients for instructional purposes. Employment Conditions The positions are full-time, nine-month, tenure-track with an expectation of summer employment paid based on credits taught and enrollment. The nine-month salary is commensurate with qualifications and rank. Application Instructions Application review will begin on October 30, 2026. Applications received after the start of review may be considered if an acceptable candidate is not identified. Applications should include: Cover letter including the candidate’s interest in the position, desired rank, interest in clinical experience leadership role, applicable qualifications, and leadership experience. Curriculum vitae (CV) including the names, phone numbers, and email addresses of three professional references from whom recommendation letters can be requested. Detailed statement of teaching philosophy. Statement of research interests; Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and email addresses for all references.) Additional materials may be requested during the review process. Specific questions related to this search should be directed to Dr. Alice McLaine at mclainea@winthrop.edu . Open Date 09/23/2026 Application Review Date 10/30/2026 Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0021P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Faculty - Statement of Research Interests Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23051": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Painting",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Painting Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Painting Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following materials in PDF Format: Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. Cover Letter (addressing how the candidate’s qualifications align with position responsibilities) Statement of Teaching Philosophy Portfolio of 20 student works (provide project brief and course name, date, materials used, etc.) Personal Artist Statement including a portfolio of 20 images of professional work (title, date, materials, short description of the work) Portfolio of up to 20 examples of the candidate’s professional, creative, or scholarly work, submitted as a PDF or URL. Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) Questions about the position and applications should be directed to Department of Department of Fine Arts, Mark Hamilton, chair, hamiltonm@winthrop.edu. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Painting Job Description Winthrop University invites applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor of Fine Arts specializing in Painting. This is a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track appointment in the Department of Fine Arts, part of the College of Visual and Performing Arts. The College also includes the departments of Design, Arts Administration, Theatre and Dance, and Music and also the Winthrop University Galleries. Winthrop’s beautiful campus is located in Rock Hill, South Carolina, within the rapidly growing Charlotte metropolitan region and approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina. The area offers a thriving arts and cultural scene as well as convenient access to Charlotte Douglas International Airport. We seek a dynamic and forward-looking artist-educator who will contribute to Winthrop’s commitment to rigorous study of image-making, material exploration, and technical excellence while helping advance the curriculum in contemporary painting practices. The successful candidate will be prepared to teach both traditional and contemporary approaches to painting using a wide range of media and substrates. Teaching responsibilities will span all undergraduate levels, from foundational studies in pictorial composition to advanced and interdisciplinary studio practices, including senior-level mentorship within the Bachelor of Fine Arts program. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach 3 studio courses each fall and spring semesters, with primary responsibility for undergraduate painting courses at the beginning, intermediate, and advanced levels. Teach foundational studio courses as needed, which may include Drawing, 2D Design, Media Studies, and related foundation-level offerings. The ability to teach introductory printmaking courses is an asset, but not required. Provide instruction in both traditional and contemporary approaches to painting, including technical proficiency with oil, acrylic, water-based, and other media; preparation and construction of painting surfaces and supports; and conceptually driven approaches to studio practice. Develop and regularly update course content to provide rigorous instruction in painting while engaging historical and contemporary practices, material exploration, technical proficiency, formal visual principles, and interdisciplinary approaches to studio art. Advise and mentor undergraduate Fine Arts majors, including supporting students in portfolio development, professional preparation, advanced studio work, and senior capstone exhibitions. Maintain an active record of creative and scholarly activity consistent with expectations for tenure and promotion, demonstrated through exhibitions, juried or curated opportunities, professional presentations, publications, commissions, residencies, or other relevant forms of professional engagement. Contribute to the continued development of the Painting area, including curriculum, teaching and learning spaces, studios, equipment, and related resources in response to evolving pedagogical and program needs. Participate in departmental and programmatic responsibilities, including curriculum development, annual assessment, accreditation activities, and other initiatives supporting the academic mission of the department. Actively support and participate in student recruitment and retention efforts, including portfolio reviews, prospective-student events, and other departmental outreach activities. Contribute professional service to the Department of Fine Arts, College of Visual and Performing Arts, University, discipline, and broader community. Support the effective and safe operation of studio facilities and equipment and maintain appropriate office hours and availability for students. Assume other responsibilities consistent with departmental needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https:/ www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Master of Fine Arts ( MFA ) in Painting or Studio Art Minimum of 2 to 3 years of successful college-level teaching experience (full-time or adjunct), supported by a portfolio of student work, personal work, and a robust personal exhibition record. Extensive mastery of traditional oil, acrylic, water-based, and mixed-media painting techniques, alongside proficiency in structural drawing and design principles. Exceptional grasp of studio pedagogy, objective grading standards, and formal design elements. Excellent verbal and written communication skills required for rigorous student evaluation and collaborative faculty governance. Preferred Qualifications Familiarity with and the ability to teach introductory printmaking courses. Employment Conditions Full time, tenure-track, 3/3 teaching load Application Instructions Applicants should submit the following materials in PDF Format: Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested. Cover Letter (addressing how the candidate’s qualifications align with position responsibilities) Statement of Teaching Philosophy Portfolio of 20 student works (provide project brief and course name, date, materials used, etc.) Personal Artist Statement including a portfolio of 20 images of professional work (title, date, materials, short description of the work) Portfolio of up to 20 examples of the candidate’s professional, creative, or scholarly work, submitted as a PDF or URL . Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) Three letters of recommendation will be requested from semifinalists at a later date. (The CV must include the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of all references.) Questions about the position and applications should be directed to Department of Department of Fine Arts, Mark Hamilton, chair, hamiltonm@winthrop.edu. Open Date 10/02/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0026P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Portfolio of Student Work Personal Artist Statement Portfolio Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
