    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22337": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Sociology",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant Professor in Sociology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant Professor in Sociology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. First consideration will be given to applications received on or before August 15, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. Letter of application that addresses the position, with statement of teaching philosophy Cover letter that includes ways the candidate can contribute to Winthrop's curriculum and a planned research trajectory; A curriculum vitae; Graduate transcripts demonstrating a terminal degree or a master's degree with at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) Letters of recommendation from three academic professionals List with contact information for three professional references Please address questions to Dr. Brad Tripp, Department Chair (trippb@winthrop.edu). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant Professor in Sociology Job Description The Winthrop University Department of Sociology, Criminology, and Anthropology invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track position beginning January 1, 2027. The successful candidate will have an active research agenda and be able to teach a variety of courses in a combined sociology program, particularly courses within the General Sociology and Social Inequalities Concentrations. Candidates will also teach Social Theory and Qualitative Research Methods. The area of specialization should allow the individual to contribute coursework in at least one of the following areas: Sociology of Technology, the User Experience (UX) research Concentration, Medical Sociology, and/or Urban Sociology. The preferred candidate will demonstrate potential for teaching and research involving students from diverse ethnic, cultural, and socioeconomic backgrounds. Requirements for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor include a Ph.D. in sociology or related field ( ABD will be considered), evidence of teaching excellence and commitment to student learning, and evidence of an ongoing program of research and publication. We seek a broadly trained teacher-scholar interested in involving undergraduate students in experiential learning. The candidate will also advise undergraduates and participate in other service to the university. The department supports a thriving B.A. program in sociology with concentrations in anthropology, social inequalities, criminology, pre-law, and User Experience (UX) Research, as well as a separate B.A. in Criminal Justice. Winthrop, a small public comprehensive university, boasts a highly diverse student body of approximately 5,000 students and has been a top-ranked regional university for more than 20 consecutive years. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach undergraduate Sociology courses, including introductory Sociology, Social Inequalities, and Social Theory/Qualitative Research Methods Develop and deliver engaging instruction using various teaching modalities Maintain a standard teaching load of 12 contact hours per semester. Provide academic advising and mentorship to undergraduate students. Develop and sustain a scholarly identity and an independent research program that will spark student interests in programs housed in the Sociology, Criminology, and Anthropology department. Participate actively in university service, including committee work, curriculum development and campus initiatives. Contribute to a supportive and inclusive learning environment that promotes student achievement and academic excellence Have strong interpersonal skills and an overall commitment to student success and engagement About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 6,000-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 01/01/2026 Required Qualifications Ph.D. in Sociology or related field is required for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor ( ABD will be considered) Experience teaching or demonstrated potential to teach sociology courses including Social Theory and Qualitative Research methods. Evidence of teaching excellence and an ongoing commitment to student learning Evidence of ongoing program of research, ideally involving undergraduates, and the potential for publication Strong oral and written communication skills Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated ability to contribute to one or more of the following areas: Sociology of Technology, the User Experience (UX) Research Concentration, Medical Sociology, and/or Urban Sociology Experience mentoring undergraduate research including, but not limited to, research lab supervision, coauthoring with students, and bringing students to conferences Interest in contributing to the major by offering a wide variety of courses including introductory courses Research that compliments existing departmental expertise and highlights the applied nature of sociology. Employment Conditions Position is full-time, nine-month, tenure-track with an expectation of summer employment. Salary is commensurate with qualifications. Application Instructions First consideration will be given to applications received on or before August 15, 2026. Applications received after the deadline may be considered if a suitable candidate has not been found. Letter of application that addresses the position, with statement of teaching philosophy Cover letter that includes ways the candidate can contribute to Winthrop’s curriculum and a planned research trajectory; A curriculum vitae; Graduate transcripts demonstrating a terminal degree or a master’s degree with at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for the initial review; official transcripts must be provided for a final appointment) Letters of recommendation from three academic professionals List with contact information for three professional references Please address questions to Dr. Brad Tripp, Department Chair (trippb@winthrop.edu). Open Date 07/02/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0002P Faculty Position Type Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty-Letter of application and statement of teaching philosophy Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Letter of Recommendation Letter of Recommendation #2 Letter of Recommendation #3 Faculty - List of References Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22303": {
      "title": "Adjunct - CAS",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct - CAS Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct - CAS Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Please send the following required materials: Cover Letter addressing teaching philosophy and relevant experience. Current Curriculum Vitae (CV). Transcripts (Unofficial documents can support the application. Official transcripts are required for the appointment) Contact information for three professional references. All questions should be directed to casga@winthrop.edu. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct - CAS Job Description The College of Arts and Sciences at Winthrop University seeks dynamic and qualified Adjunct Faculty to teach in disciplines across its 12 academic departments. The ideal candidate will have a demonstrated commitment to high-quality, student-centered instruction that is informed by strong academic credentials and real-world experiences. Applicants should be passionate about fostering student success in an engaging learning environment. Position Responsibilities Adjunct faculty, part-time appointments, are expected to adhere to all policies and procedures expected of university employees. Adjunct faculty will: Offer evidence of effective Student Intellectual Development activities Demonstrate a commitment to meeting the academic responsibility requirements for all faculty (e.g., adherence to academic policies, participating in the collection of assessment data necessary for course and program evaluation). Attend any College orientation sessions. Develop and distribute a written course syllabus as outlined by university policy and College policy at the first course meeting. Present course content as announced to students and as approved by the faculty. Meet all assigned classes or make appropriate alternative arrangements for the class when an absence is unavoidable. Schedule and maintain regular office hours (in-person or virtual) and be available to students. Award academic credit based on the professional evaluation of academic performance. Observe the policies and procedures of the department, College, and the University. Administer student evaluations and any additional assessment instruments required by the University. File an annual report that provides evidence of progress and involvement that is responsive to feedback and meets expectations of rank. About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 6,000-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2026 Required Qualifications Successful candidates must have a master’s degree and a minimum of 18 graduate credit hours in the field of instruction. Please specify in the application portal which subject you are qualified to teach given these criteria. Preferred Qualifications Previous experience teaching undergraduate students and proficiency with instructional technology and Learning Management Systems (e.g., Canvas, Blackboard) are preferred. Employment Conditions Position is part-time, adjunct Application Instructions Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Please send the following required materials: Cover Letter addressing teaching philosophy and relevant experience. Current Curriculum Vitae (CV). Transcripts (Unofficial documents can support the application. Official transcripts are required for the appointment) Contact information for three professional references. All questions should be directed to casga@winthrop.edu. Open Date 08/01/2026 Application Review Date 08/01/2026 Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0004P Faculty Position Type Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty - List of References Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22240": {
      "title": "Adjunct - Instructor of Dance",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct - Instructor of Dance Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct - Instructor of Dance Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Please send the following required materials: Cover Letter Current Curriculum Vitae (CV). Transcripts (Unofficial documents can support the application. Official transcripts are required for the appointment) Contact information for three professional references. For more information, please contact: Daniel Gordon Chair and Professor Department of Theatre & Dance gordond@winthrop.edu and/or Kelly Ozust Associate Professor of Dance and Dance Program Director ozustk@winthrop.edu Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct - Instructor of Dance Job Description Teach contemporary and/or jazz and/or ballet technique at all levels. Position Responsibilities Teach contemporary and/or jazz and/or ballet technique at all levels. Opportunity to choreograph for departmental musical theatre of dance productions. Teach other technique courses and dance theory courses based on expertise/interest. About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 6,000-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2026 Required Qualifications Expertise and interest to teach contemporary and/or jazz and/or ballet technique at all levels Experience choreographing dance or musical theatre productions at the college level Evidence of effective interpersonal skills Evidence of proven success working in a team-oriented environment Adjunct faculty teaching undergraduate courses at Winthrop University are expected to possess at least 18 hours of graduate credits in the discipline. In cases of exceptional and significant professional experiences, the said experience may be considered in lieu of these credits Preferred Qualifications B.A. or M.F.A. in dance Employment Conditions Position is part-time, adjunct Application Instructions Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Please send the following required materials: Cover Letter Current Curriculum Vitae (CV). Transcripts (Unofficial documents can support the application. Official transcripts are required for the appointment) Contact information for three professional references. For more information, please contact: Daniel Gordon Chair and Professor Department of Theatre & Dance gordond@winthrop.edu and/or Kelly Ozust Associate Professor of Dance and Dance Program Director ozustk@winthrop.edu Open Date 07/21/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0005P Faculty Position Type Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty - List of References Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/22238": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor in Design",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct Instructor in Design Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct Instructor in Design Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Interested individuals are encouraged to submit their credentials as soon as possible for full consideration. Applications should include: A cover letter addressing interest in and qualifications for the position that also includes a statement of teaching philosophy. A current curriculum vitae. Portfolio that includes evidence of teaching excellence (narrative with teaching philosophy) with images of student work and descriptions (URL or PDF). Media file of up to 20 images of the candidate's creative, corporate, and/or scholarly work (URL or PDF). Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts acceptable at this stage, but official transcripts needed for final appointment). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct Instructor in Design Job Description Winthrop University, a highly ranked, public, comprehensive university, announces a search for adjunct instructors of design, teaching for the Department of Design within the College of Visual and Performing Arts. Our campus is in Rock Hill, South Carolina, just thirty minutes from Charlotte North Carolina’s thriving design and art market and international airport. This position requires the instructor to teach undergraduate courses, such as Design Fundamentals ( DESF ), Interior Design ( INDS ), or Visual Communication ( VCOM ). Candidates should demonstrate commitment to an interdisciplinary and collaborative approach to design studies, at the foundation, intermediate, and advanced levels. The ideal candidate will demonstrate expertise in design practices including but not limited to game design, graphic design, illustration, and and/or interior design, as well as design research, design thinking, interactive and web design, and interactive digital products. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to high-quality undergraduate teaching, student engagement, and creating a positive learning environment. Position Responsibilities Teach assigned class or classes in the undergraduate design degree, such as studio courses (6 contact hours per week) or lecture (3 contact hours per week), or seminars (from 1-hour to 2.5-hours per week) Prepare course content using a variety of methods Deliver assigned course materials, projects, and examinations consistent with departmental learning outcomes Promote student learning and participation Provide reasonable support to students outside of class meeting times, maintaining office hours on campus, or via video, or other approved means Maintain course records, submit grades by university deadlines, and comply with university academic policies About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 6,000-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2026 Required Qualifications Candidates should hold a terminal degree ( MFA , MDes,) in Design or a related discipline. Candidates holding a bachelor’s degree in design—such as a BA or BFA or BDes, or degree other than an MFA or MDes in design—who have significant teaching and/or professional experience equivalent to a terminal degree, may be considered. Have a demonstrated ability to communicate effectively and work with diverse student populations, and a commitment to excellence in undergraduate teaching and student engagement Preferred Qualifications Minimum 2–3 years of university-level teaching experience, which could include graduate-level and adjunct teaching. Minimum of 5 years of professional experience in industry. Drawing, drafting, and print production are necessary for our foundations and some upper-level courses. Familiarity with Adobe Creative Cloud, knowledge of printing pre-press principles and printing methodology, and knowledge of user experience design and product design, with an ability to use and demonstrate use of Photoshop, Illustrator, and InDesign. Interior design instructors should know AutoCad, Revit, SketchUp, codes and standards, and contract documents, as well as rendering techniques with markers. Employment Conditions Position is non-tenure, adjunct position. Application Instructions Review of applicants will begin immediately and continue throughout the 2026-27 academic year. Interested individuals are encouraged to submit their credentials as soon as possible for full consideration. Applications should include: A cover letter addressing interest in and qualifications for the position that also includes a statement of teaching philosophy. A current curriculum vitae. Portfolio that includes evidence of teaching excellence (narrative with teaching philosophy) with images of student work and descriptions ( URL or PDF ). Media file of up to 20 images of the candidate’s creative, corporate, and/or scholarly work ( URL or PDF ). Transcript and evidence of highest degree achieved (unofficial transcripts acceptable at this stage, but official transcripts needed for final appointment). Open Date 07/01/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2026FC0009P Faculty Position Type Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Portfolio Media File Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23265": {
      "title": "Adjunct - CESHS",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct - CESHS Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct - CESHS Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should identify in the letter of interest the area(s) of expertise and whether they are interested in teaching, supervision, or both. Additional materials may be requested as part of the review process. Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct - CESHS Job Description The Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences seeks qualified adjunct faculty to support Educator Preparation Programs and Educational Leadership. Adjunct faculty may be assigned to teach undergraduate and/or graduate courses, supervise teacher candidates in clinical experiences, based on program needs and the individual’s qualifications and area of expertise. Teaching assignments may support Early Childhood Education, Elementary Education, Middle Level Education, Special Education, Physical Education, the Education Core, Educational Leadership, or related educator preparation coursework. Assignments may be delivered in face-to-face, online, hybrid, field-based, or school-based settings. Position Responsibilities •Teach assigned courses in accordance with approved course syllabi, program expectations, university policies, and applicable professional and accreditation standards. • Deliver engaging, student-centered instruction that supports course and program learning outcomes and connects theory, research, and professional practice. • Maintain regular communication with students; provide timely feedback; evaluate performance fairly; maintain accurate records; and submit grades and required reports by established deadlines. • Foster a professional, respectful, inclusive, and collaborative learning environment. • For supervision assignments, support candidates in field-based and clinical experiences and serve as a liaison among the candidate, school-based mentor/partner, and university. • Conduct required observations, conferences, evaluations, and documentation associated with assigned clinical experiences and provide constructive feedback that supports candidate growth. • Collaborate with school partners, program faculty, and other university personnel to support candidate development and program expectations. • Complete required university, EPP , and/or supervisor training and use designated systems for assessment, documentation, and reporting. • Respond promptly to department, college, EPP , and university communications and participate in required meetings or training as assigned. • Comply with university policies and procedures related to instruction, academic integrity, accessibility, student privacy, and professional conduct. About the College To learn more about the Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/ceshs/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Not applicable Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability Required Qualifications • Master’s degree in teacher education, curriculum and instruction, educational leadership, the assigned teaching discipline, or a closely related field for undergraduate teaching, with appropriate graduate preparation in the discipline; earned doctorate in the discipline or a closely related field for graduate-level teaching. • Demonstrated expertise and professional preparation appropriate to the assigned course, program area, and/or supervision assignment. • Public school teaching experience or other relevant PK–12 professional experience appropriate to the assignment. • Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. • Ability to work collaboratively with university colleagues, school partners, mentor teachers, administrators, and candidates. • Ability to effectively teach and/or supervise in the assigned face-to-face, online, hybrid, field-based, or school-based environment. • Commitment to student and candidate success, excellence in teaching, and professional practice. • For supervision assignments, current or previous state educator licensure/certification appropriate to the area of supervision and successful professional experience in the area being supervised. Preferred Qualifications • Teaching experience in higher education. • Experience supervising teacher candidates, interns, student teachers, or other clinical/field-based experiences. • Experience teaching in online or hybrid environments and using instructional technology. • Current or recent PK–12 teaching or school leadership experience relevant to the assignment. • SCTS 4.0 Teacher Evaluator Training for individuals assigned to teacher-candidate supervision. Candidates who have not completed this training may be trained prior to beginning applicable supervision responsibilities. • For Educational Leadership assignments, professional experience in school or district leadership appropriate to the assigned course or program responsibilities. Employment Conditions Position is part-time, non-tenure-track, adjunct. Application Instructions Applicants should identify in the letter of interest the area(s) of expertise and whether they are interested in teaching, supervision, or both. Additional materials may be requested as part of the review process. Open Date 10/28/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0040P Faculty Position Type Adjunct Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Transcripts Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents License or Certificate IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23261": {
      "title": "Instructor/Associate Director, Practicum Education",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Instructor/Associate Director, Practicum Education Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Instructor/Associate Director, Practicum Education Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants must submit: Letter of application, including teaching philosophy Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested Copies of all graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Instructor/Associate Director, Practicum Education Job Description The Department of Social Work at Winthrop University invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track Instructor of Social Work/Associate Director of Practicum Education. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching, student engagement, professional social work education, and the preparation of competent, ethical, and culturally responsive social work practitioners. Additionally, the successful candidate will provide leadership and administrative support for the planning, coordination, implementation, assessment, and continuous improvement of practicum education within the Bachelor of Social Work ( BSW ) and Master of Social Work ( MSW ) programs. The position requires a commitment to student-centered teaching, inclusive educational practices, and integration of social work theory, research, policy, and practice. Position Responsibilities Teach undergraduate and graduate Social Work courses. Deliver instruction in face-to-face, hybrid, and online learning environments. Provide academic advising and mentoring to undergraduate and graduate social work students. Attend and participate in departmental meetings, faculty development activities, and other assigned university responsibilities. Participate in community engagement initiatives and maintain collaborative relationships with social work practitioners and community organizations. Assist the Director of Practicum Education in the overall administration and coordination of practicum education for undergraduate and graduate social work students. Coordinate practicum education activities across traditional, advanced standing, online, and other approved program delivery formats. Establish, cultivate, and maintain relationships with social service agencies, healthcare organizations, schools, government agencies, and community-based organizations. Identify and recruit new practicum placement sites that provide meaningful learning opportunities aligned with program competencies. Assist with the development, review, and maintenance of agency affiliation agreements. Provide orientation, training, consultation, and ongoing support to agency-based practicum instructors and supervisors. Serve as a liaison between the Department of Social Work and community partners to promote effective collaboration and address practicum-related concerns. Assist BSW and MSW students in identifying practicum placements appropriate to their educational preparation, professional interests, and learning needs. Demonstrate a commitment to collegiality, shared governance, and the mission of Winthrop University. About the College To learn more about the College of Arts & Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cas/ About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 01/01/2027 Required Qualifications Master of Social Work ( MSW ) degree from a CSWE -accredited social work program. Minimum of two years of post- MSW professional social work practice experience. Minimum of two years of practicum education experience at a CSWE -accredited social work program. Demonstrated knowledge of social work theory, practice, policy, research, and professional ethics. Effective written, oral, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to teach and mentor students from diverse educational and experiential backgrounds. Experience with practicum placement management systems, learning management systems, and electronic student records. Demonstrated experience with program assessment, accreditation reporting, and continuous quality improvement. Experience developing community partnerships and coordinating student placements across multiple service settings. Preferred Qualifications Previous teaching experience in higher education, particularly in a CSWE -accredited BSW or MSW program. Experience developing and delivering online and hybrid courses. Previous experience coordinating or administering practicum education in a CSWE -accredited BSW or MSW program. Experience serving as an agency-based practicum instructor, faculty practicum liaison, or practicum education administrator. Employment Conditions This is a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-track faculty appointment with additional summer responsibilities supported by a separate summer stipend. Application Instructions Applicants must submit: Letter of application, including teaching philosophy Curriculum vitae (CV), including the names, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three (3) professional references from whom letters of recommendation have been requested Copies of all graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) Open Date 10/28/2026 Application Review Date Close Date 11/02/2026 Posting Number 2027FC0039P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty-Letter of application and statement of teaching philosophy Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23259": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Counseling and Development",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct, Counseling and Development Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct, Counseling and Development Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Positions are filled on an as needed basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Applications must include: Letter of interest Curriculum vitae (CV) Copies of all graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) List of references Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct, Counseling and Development Job Description The Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences seeks qualified adjunct faculty to support the Counseling and Development program. Adjunct faculty may be assigned to teach graduate courses, supervise school counselors and clinical mental health counselors in training in clinical experiences, based on program needs and the individual’s qualifications and area of expertise. Assignments may be delivered in face-to-face, online, hybrid, field-based, or school-based settings. Position Responsibilities • Teach assigned courses in accordance with approved course syllabi, program expectations, university policies, and applicable professional and accreditation standards. • Deliver engaging, student-centered instruction that supports course and program learning outcomes and connects theory, research, and professional practice. • Maintain regular communication with students; provide timely feedback; evaluate performance fairly; maintain accurate records; and submit grades and required reports by established deadlines. • Foster a professional, respectful, inclusive, and collaborative learning environment. • For supervision assignments, support counselors in training in field-based and clinical experiences and serve as a liaison among the candidate, school or community-based mentor/partner, and university. • Conduct required supervision, evaluations, and documentation associated with assigned clinical experiences and provide constructive feedback that supports counselor in training growth. • Collaborate with school/community site partners, program faculty, and other university personnel to support candidate development and program expectations. • Complete required university, and/or supervisor training and use designated systems for assessment, documentation, and reporting. • Respond promptly to department, college, and university communications and participate in required meetings or training as assigned. • Comply with university policies and procedures related to instruction, academic integrity, accessibility, student privacy, and professional conduct. About the College To learn more about the Richard W. Riley College of Education, Sport, and Human Sciences please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/ceshs/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Not applicable Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability Required Qualifications • Master’s or doctoral degree in counseling, the assigned teaching discipline, or a closely related field with appropriate graduate preparation in the discipline; earned doctorate in the discipline or a closely related field for graduate-level teaching. • Demonstrated expertise and professional preparation appropriate to the assigned course, program area, and/or supervision assignment. • Counseling supervision experience appropriate to the assignment. • Strong written, verbal, interpersonal, and presentation skills. • Ability to work collaboratively with university colleagues, school partners, mentor teachers, administrators, and candidates. • Ability to effectively teach and/or supervise in the assigned face-to-face, online, hybrid, field-based, or school-based environment. • Commitment to student and candidate success, excellence in teaching, and professional practice. • For supervision assignments, current or previous state licensure appropriate to the area of supervision and successful professional experience in the area being supervised. Preferred Qualifications • Teaching experience in higher education. • Experience supervising counselors in training, or other clinical/field-based experiences. • Experience teaching in online or hybrid environments and using instructional technology. • Current or recent counseling experience relevant to the assignment. Employment Conditions Position is part-time, non-tenure-track, adjunct. Application Instructions Positions are filled on an as needed basis for the 2026-2027 academic year. Applications must include: Letter of interest Curriculum vitae (CV) Copies of all graduate transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment) List of references Open Date 10/28/2026 Application Review Date Close Date 05/10/2027 Posting Number 2027FC0038P Faculty Position Type Adjunct Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty - Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty - List of References Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23252": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Accounting",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct, Accounting Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct, Accounting Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applications will be reviewed as they are received and until position is filled. Successful applications should include: Curriculum vitae Transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct, Accounting Job Description The Department of Accounting, Finance, and Economics invites applications for an Adjunct Faculty member to teach ACCT 521 Fraud and Forensics. The ideal candidate will bring relevant academic or professional experience and demonstrate a commitment to student-centered instruction. Position Responsibilities Teach ACCT 521 Fraud and Forensics in accordance with established course objectives and departmental expectations. Develop and deliver course materials, assignments, assessments, and learning activities that support achievement of course learning outcomes. Incorporate relevant professional examples into the course when appropriate. Provide timely and constructive feedback to students and maintain appropriate communication with students throughout the semester. Maintain accurate course records and submit grades and other required materials in accordance with university policies and deadlines. About the College To learn more about the College of Business & Technology please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cbt/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Not applicable Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 01/01/2027 Required Qualifications Master’s in Business Administration, Accounting, or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Ability to effectively teach and engage undergraduate students. Professional experience and professional certification, such as CPA or CFE . Preferred Qualifications Employment Conditions Position is part-time, non-tenure-track, adjunct. Application Instructions Applications will be reviewed as they are received and until position is filled. Successful applications should include: Curriculum vitae Transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). Open Date 10/28/2026 Application Review Date Close Date 11/01/2026 Posting Number 2027FC0037P Faculty Position Type Adjunct Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23238": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit the following materials in a pdf: Cover letter (addressing qualifications and why candidate is a good fit for the position) CV Statement of Teaching Philosophy (one page) Artist Statement 20 images of professional work (for video work please include link to video sample) Professional portfolio image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) 10-15 images of student work (for video work please include link to video sample) Student work image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) List 3 professional References (reference will be contacted upon final round of interview process) Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Assistant/Associate Professor, Ceramics Job Description Winthrop University invites applications for an Assistant or Associate Professor of Fine Arts specializing in Ceramics. This full-time, nine-month, tenure-track position is housed in the Department of Fine Arts within the College of Visual and Performing Arts, which also includes the departments of Design, Arts Administration, Theatre and Dance, Music, and the Winthrop University Galleries. Winthrop is located in Rock Hill, South Carolina, approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina. The growing Charlotte metropolitan region offers a vibrant arts and cultural community and convenient access to Charlotte Douglas International Airport. We seek a dynamic, forward-thinking artist-educator committed to material exploration, technical excellence, and contemporary ceramic practices. The successful candidate will bring expertise in both traditional and contemporary approaches to ceramics, with the ability to teach a broad range of techniques, from functional wheel-thrown vessels to large-scale sculptural forms. The position combines teaching, curriculum development, studio management, and program coordination. The teaching load is three courses per semester (3/3), plus BFA student advising. Teaching responsibilities span all undergraduate levels, from foundational ceramics to advanced studio practices, including mentorship of senior-level Bachelor of Fine Arts students. As the sole faculty member in ceramics, the successful candidate will lead curriculum development and oversee studio facilities, equipment maintenance, and safe studio operations. Candidates should demonstrate an active, nationally or internationally recognized studio practice through regular exhibitions, gallery representation, or juried shows. At least three years of teaching experience in higher education is preferred. Position Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Teach three studio courses each fall and spring semester, with primary responsibility for two ceramics courses and one course in either foundations or jewelry/metals. Provide instruction in traditional and contemporary approaches to ceramics, including wheel throwing, hand-building, functional pottery, sculptural forms, surface treatments, glazing, and firing processes. Develop and regularly update course content to provide rigorous instruction in ceramics while engaging historical and contemporary practices, material exploration, technical proficiency, conceptual development, and interdisciplinary approaches to studio art. Advise and mentor undergraduate Fine Arts majors, including supporting students in portfolio development, professional preparation, advanced studio work, and senior capstone exhibitions. Maintain an active record of creative and scholarly activity consistent with expectations for tenure and promotion, demonstrated through exhibitions, juried or curated opportunities, professional presentations, publications, commissions, residencies, or other relevant forms of professional engagement. Oversee and maintain the ceramics facilities, including teaching studios, indoor kiln room, clay mixing room, glaze mixing room, and outdoor kiln area, ensuring safe operation, equipment maintenance, and appropriate studio practices. Supervise student employees and coordinate responsibilities related to studio maintenance, organization, and upkeep. Lead the continued development of the Ceramics area, including curriculum, facilities, equipment, and related resources in response to evolving pedagogical and program needs. Participate in departmental and programmatic responsibilities, including curriculum development, annual assessment, accreditation activities, and other initiatives supporting the academic mission of the department. Actively support student recruitment and retention efforts, including portfolio reviews, prospective-student events, and departmental outreach activities. Contribute professional service to the Department of Fine Arts, College of Visual and Performing Arts, University, discipline, and broader community, demonstrating collegiality, professionalism, and ethical conduct. Maintain appropriate office hours and availability for students and assume other responsibilities consistent with departmental needs and the candidate’s areas of expertise. About the College To learn more about the College of Visual & Performing Arts please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cvpa/ . About Winthrop University Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits At Winthrop University, we are proud to offer a comprehensive benefits package that significantly enhances the total compensation for our employees. In partnership with the State of South Carolina and the Public Employee Benefit Authority ( PEBA ), our benefits include health and life insurance, generous paid leave, parental leave, and retirement programs. These benefits reflect our commitment to supporting the well-being and financial security of our employees. For more information please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/hr/employee-benefits.aspx . Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 08/16/2027 Required Qualifications Master of Fine Arts ( MFA ) Degree in related area Preferred Qualifications Demonstrated knowledge of historical and contemporary ceramic practices and related three-dimensional art and design disciplines. Successful experience teaching ceramics at the undergraduate level. An active and sustained creative and scholarly practice in ceramics, demonstrated through exhibitions and other forms of professional engagement. Strong organizational, management, and communication skills. Experience managing and maintaining safe ceramics facilities, including expertise in kiln operation, firing processes, glaze formulation, and studio equipment. Knowledge of best practices in ceramics studio safety, including silica exposure prevention, material handling, ventilation, and studio housekeeping. Ability to teach foundational three-dimensional design courses or courses in jewelry and metalsmithing. Employment Conditions Full time, tenure-track, 3/3 teaching load Application Instructions Applicants should submit the following materials in a pdf: Cover letter (addressing qualifications and why candidate is a good fit for the position) CV Statement of Teaching Philosophy (one page) Artist Statement 20 images of professional work (for video work please include link to video sample) Professional portfolio image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) 10-15 images of student work (for video work please include link to video sample) Student work image list (title, date, materials, short description of work) List 3 professional References (reference will be contacted upon final round of interview process) Graduate degree transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for application but official transcripts will be required for an offer of employment). Open Date 10/14/2026 Application Review Date Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0029P Faculty Position Type Faculty Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Faculty - Statement of Teaching Philosophy Personal Artist Statement Images of Professional Work Professional Portfolio Image List Student Work Image List Faculty - List of References Faculty-All transcripts Optional Documents Images of Student Work Faculty - Portfolio of Student Work (file) IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://winthrop.peopleadmin.com/postings/23222": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Computing and Information Sciences",
      "description": "Winthrop University Applicant Portal | Adjunct, Computing and Information Sciences Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Log In /Create Account Help Human Resources Adjunct, Computing and Information Sciences Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Applicants should submit: Letter of interest describing their qualifications and relevant teaching or professional experience Current curriculum vitae All graduate transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). Contact information for three professional references. Review of applicants will begin on November 2, 2026, and continues until the position is filled. For more information, please see http://www.winthrop.edu/hr/ Position Details Position Information Winthrop Position Title Adjunct, Computing and Information Sciences Job Description The Department of Computer and Information Sciences at Winthrop University invites applications for a part-time adjunct faculty position to teach undergraduate courses in computing. The initial teaching assignment will be CSCI 101 – Introduction to Computing. The successful candidate will introduce students to fundamental computing concepts, applications, and technologies while helping them develop practical computing skills. Additional teaching opportunities may be available based on departmental needs and the candidate’s qualifications. Position Responsibilities Teach assigned undergraduate computing courses, initially CSCI 101 – Introduction to Computing. Prepare and deliver course materials, assignments, and assessments consistent with departmental learning objectives. Evaluate student performance and provide timely, constructive feedback. Maintain accurate student records and communicate regularly with students regarding their academic progress. Hold office hours and provide appropriate academic support to students. Follow university and departmental policies regarding instruction, grading, and academic integrity. About the College To learn more about the College of Business & Technology please visit: https://www.winthrop.edu/cbt/ . About Winthrop University 5Founded in 1886, Winthrop University in Rock Hill, South Carolina, offers its 5,200-plus undergraduate and graduate students an educational experience that blends liberal arts, professional programs, global awareness, and civic engagement. In its colleges of Arts and Sciences; Business and Technology; Education, Sport, and Human Sciences; and Visual and Performing Arts, Winthrop embraces only those programs and activities that can be delivered at an exemplary level. Winthrop University is achieving national stature as a competitive and distinctive co-educational, public, residential, comprehensive, values-oriented institution. The values of service, excellence, community, and leadership provide the foundation for Winthrop’s continuing development and shape Winthrop’s continuing success. Numerous national accolades reflect the sustained high quality and value of the Winthrop Experience, including the most recent U.S. News & World Report ’s ranking of Winthrop as one of the top-10 public comprehensive universities in the South. Winthrop is an active member of the Rock Hill community and recognized as a key component of the region’s economic resurgence. Approximately 20 minutes from Charlotte, North Carolina, Winthrop has direct access to and is engaged in a broad and expansive regional business community. Winthrop’s beautiful campus encompasses a rich architectural blend of neo-Georgian buildings and is included in the National Register of Historic Places. The attractive recreational and research complex area that surrounds Winthrop Lake is home to Winthrop’s 18 Division I men’s and women’s sports. Benefits Not applicable Background Check Winthrop University is committed to providing a safe campus community. Winthrop conducts background investigations for applicants being considered for employment. Background investigations include reference checks, a criminal history record check, and when appropriate, a financial (credit) report or driving history check. Position Availability 01/01/2027 Required Qualifications Master’s degree in Computer Science, Information Technology, Information Systems, or a closely related field; or a master’s degree with at least 18 graduate credit hours in an appropriate computing discipline; or significant professional experience in computing or information technology. Demonstrated knowledge of fundamental computing concepts and commonly used computing technologies. Effective written and oral communication skills. Ability to work effectively with students from diverse academic backgrounds and levels of computing experience. Preferred Qualifications Previous teaching experience at the college or university level. Experience teaching introductory computing or computer literacy courses. Experience using learning management systems and instructional technologies. Professional experience in computing, information technology, or a related field. Employment Conditions This is a part-time, non-tenure-track adjunct faculty appointment with teaching assignments determined on a semester-by-semester basis according to departmental needs and course availability. The initial assignment is anticipated to be CSCI 101 – Introduction to Computing. Additional teaching assignments may be offered depending on qualifications and departmental needs. Application Instructions Applicants should submit: Letter of interest describing their qualifications and relevant teaching or professional experience Current curriculum vitae All graduate transcripts of coursework that includes at least 18hrs in the discipline (unofficial transcripts are acceptable for initial review; official transcripts are required prior to final appointment). Contact information for three professional references. Review of applicants will begin on November 2, 2026, and continues until the position is filled. For more information, please see http://www.winthrop.edu/hr/ Open Date 10/26/2026 Application Review Date 11/02/2026 Close Date Posting Number 2027FC0035P Faculty Position Type Adjunct Documents Needed to Apply Required Documents Faculty - Letter of Interest Curriculum Vitae Faculty-All transcripts Faculty - List of References Optional Documents IN THE HEART OF THE CAROLINAS © Winthrop University · 701 Oakland Avenue · Rock Hill, SC 29733, USA · 803/323-2211 To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
